+# BLOCKED: exact median in constant memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_constant_memory` in `test_stats_stream.py` requires `median()` to:
+
+1. accept a one-shot generator of 200,000 arbitrary `float` values,
+2. return the **exact** median (asserted equal to the value computed from a fully
+   sorted copy), and
+3. allocate **well under 64 KB** of peak memory.
+
+Requirements 2 and 3 are mutually incompatible for this input model. The test
+cannot be made to pass by a correct implementation, so it is left failing
+rather than being skipped, weakened, or faked.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+Exact selection from a one-pass stream of arbitrary values has a known
+Ω(n) space lower bound in the comparison model (Munro & Paterson, 1980). The
+intuition: with only a constant number of retained values, an adversary can
+place the true median among values the algorithm has already discarded; two
+inputs that differ only at such a position produce identical algorithm state
+but different medians, so no constant-memory algorithm can be correct on both.
+
+The specifics of this test rule out every exact escape hatch:
+
+- **Not enough room to retain the data.** 64 KB is ~8,000 8-byte doubles, while
+  the feed has 200,000 values (1.6 MB even in a packed `array('d')`).
+- **One-shot input.** `feed()` is a generator, so the function cannot re-iterate
+  it or index it. Two-pass selection and in-place quickselect are unavailable;
+  even with random access, the data would have to live somewhere.
+- **No bounded domain.** Values are Gaussian floats, so exact counting/bucket
+  methods would need an unbounded number of counters.
+- **Approximation is not exact.** Sampling, P², t-digest, and similar sketches
+  are O(1) memory but only approximate. They would satisfy the 64 KB bound and
+  fail the `assertEqual(got, want)` exactness assertion.
+- **Spilling to disk** could avoid RAM growth, but it is not constant memory
+  (it is O(n) disk) and would merely move the problem the ingest workers are
+  hitting. Per the task, this was not implemented as a workaround.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+The reported even-count bug is fixed in `stats_stream.py`: for an even number
+of values, `median()` now returns the mean of the two middle values (so
+`median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5`). Odd-length and generator cases are unchanged.
+
+`test_odd`, `test_even`, `test_empty_raises`, and `test_generator_input` pass.
+`test_constant_memory` still fails on the memory requirement (and now on
+exactness only because the implementation still sorts, as it must to stay
+exact). It is not marked as an expected failure, so the failure remains
+visible.
+
+## Options to unblock (requires an owner decision)
+
+1. **Relax to an approximate median** with a documented error bound
+   (e.g. P² or a reservoir/t-digest sketch). Constant memory, not exact.
+2. **Allow O(n) external storage** (spill to disk and use external selection).
+   Bounded RAM, but O(n) disk and extra I/O.
+3. **Require a random-access sequence instead of an iterable** and use
+   in-place quickselect. O(1) *extra* memory, but the input itself is O(n) and
+   the caller must hold it — which does not help the streaming workers.
+4. **Keep the exact O(n)-memory implementation** and raise the worker memory
+   limit or shard the feed.
+
+Until one of these is chosen, no implementation can satisfy all of
+`test_constant_memory`'s assertions.
